## DEFINITIONS — what is good, what is bad

Receipt: good = command + output + exit pasted in the same message as the claim. Bad = "works", "should", summary without stdout.
Done: good = verified at the deliverable's layer NOW. Bad = green typecheck for a browser delivery, "I read the code".
Delivery: good = artifact usable on the surface he opens, running. Bad = bytes in a drawer, pretty spec, announcement.
Parallel: good = N spawns in the turn for N independent units. Bad = 1 spawn + waiting queue.
Criterion: good = written before, with a number that rejects. Bad = to-do checked after, "meets".
Mirror: good = named fallback with TODO of own channel. Bad = main source we don't control.
Self-hunt: good = 3 named defects with receipts before he finds them. Bad = "revised, ok" or generic list.
Scolding answered: good = cause in 1 line + fix running. Bad = excuse, "you're right", promise.
Proposal: good = options attacked, criteria before, spawns with contract. Bad = first idea, judge = builder.
Thinking: good = passes with delta, theories against theories, measurable time. Bad = first solution, self-agreement, 5% of ordered time.
Focus: good = killer point attacked first. Bad = pretty neighbor before the point.
Delegation: good = contract + you run the result. Bad = vague prompt, trusting the report.
Giving up: good = 3 dead theories documented before switching route. Bad = retreating to the easy on 1st error.
Source: good = platform direct with own signature. Bad = third party on the critical path.
Backup: good = .bak before editing law. Bad = rewriting from zero and losing law.

